0380: SapphireGpu, pmap: a process's own memory as a GPU buffer (msm request 0x7d), its caches maintained by the pages a CPU wrote (kern.sweet_gpu_usermem, default 1)

Needs 0336, 0339 and 0342. What a Metal driver needs for -newBufferWithBytesNoCopy: to be what Metal says: the
caller's pages themselves. QuartzCore 20H392 wraps an image's own pages with -newBufferWithBytesNoCopy: and
keeps a texture over them whenever its pixels and row bytes are multiples of
deviceLinearReadOnlyTextureAlignmentBytes (64), and does not upload that image again; a driver that copies
the bytes at creation shows the image as it was then (measured: widgets with an opaque background drawn
empty).

1. Request 0x7d (struct msm_user_memory: address, size, flags 0; returns a handle): the client task's range,
   whole pages, at most 256 MB, as a buffer, via IOMemoryDescriptor::withAddressRange and
   prepare(kIODirectionInOut): the pages are wired until the buffer is freed (after the last submission that
   named it) and marked dirty for the VM. Only pmap-managed pages; a client holds at most 512 MB of it. It is
   not mapped through the client (clientMemoryForType refuses it). GET_PARAM 0x1001 answers 1 (0 with
   kern.sweet_gpu_usermem=0, which also refuses the request).
2. Caches. The GPU is not coherent with the CPU caches (the stock kgsl SMMU node has no dma-coherent) and
   these pages are cacheable. Cleaning them all at every submission would cost 2-9 ms per submission for
   QuartzCore's 7-29 MB of client-storage images (at the ~3 GB/s 0336 measured). So each page keeps an
   account in the pmap: pmap_test_and_clear_modify() (osfmk/arm/pmap/pmap.c, new) answers whether any
   mapping wrote the page since it was last asked and, if so, write-protects it again and clears the bit, in
   one step under the page's PV lock. Then a submission that reads the buffer cleans (dc cvac) each page
   written since; one that writes it (MSM_SUBMIT_BO_WRITE, or neither flag) cleans and invalidates (dc civac)
   them, and once it is done the whole buffer is cleaned and invalidated again before the client reads it (at
   its first WAIT_FENCE or CPU_PREP that finds it done, or when it is freed). Maintenance is by line through
   the kernel's physical map. When a buffer is freed each page's modified bit is set again for the VM.
3. kern.sweet_gpu_usermem_track=0 (boot-arg gpu_usermem_track=0) maintains every page at every submission,
   to hold the account against. kern.sweet_gpu_usermem_stats: buffers made, refused, freed, bytes held and
   peak, pages looked at, cleaned, invalidated, and times.

Not covered: a CPU writing the pages while the GPU reads them (nothing orders that, in Metal either); a page
wired in the pmap (mlock) cannot be write-protected and is maintained every time. With XNU_MONITOR (not this
configuration) pmap_test_and_clear_modify answers TRUE.

Checked: clang -fsyntax-only of SapphireGpu.cpp and pmap.c with stub headers, with an undeclared identifier
put into every new function and reported for each.
Measured on the phone (one boot, with a Metal driver that uses it, home, Today view, Control Center, app
switcher, 6 app launches): made 34, refused 22, freed 24, peak 393216 bytes held; 3521 pages looked at, 8
cleaned; the opaque widgets are drawn. Off: sysctl -w kern.sweet_gpu_usermem=0 (or boot-arg gpu_usermem=0):
the driver then gets no no-copy buffers and QuartzCore uploads its images.

